<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tassili n'Ajjer</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tassili n'Ajjer is a sandstone plateau in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, near the Libyan border, in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. It is famous for thousands of prehistoric rock paintings and engravings showing wild animals, herds and people from a time when the Sahara was green. It is a World Heritage Site and the homeland of the Kel Ajjer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tassili-najjer.jpg" alt="Tassili n'Ajjer" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heribertus2 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Tassili_n%27Ajjer_and_surroundings-en.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tassili n'Ajjer, meaning plateau of rivers, is a mountain range and plateau in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> at the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, covering more than 72,000 square kilometres. Its highest point is Adrar Afao, at 2,158 metres, and the nearest towns are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djanet">Djanet</a> and Illizi. The range is mostly sandstone stained by desert varnish, and erosion has formed rock forests resembling a lunar landscape, nearly 300 natural rock arches in the south-east, deep gorges and permanent pools in the north.</p><p>Because of its height and the water-retaining sandstone, vegetation is more lush than in the surrounding desert, with open woodland of endangered Saharan cypress and Saharan myrtle. It is a national park and Biosphere Reserve and was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage list in 1982.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The highlands show occupation from 5500 to 1500 BCE, mainly by transhumant pastoralists of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> who camped there in the dry season, while hearths and stone tumuli in the lowlands date between 6000 and 4000 BCE. Hunter-gatherers lived in the region between about 10,000 and 7500 years ago, and after 7500 years ago people organised into pastoral groups in response to an increasingly unpredictable climate.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">Cattle</a> were herded across wide areas as early as 3000 to 2000 BCE, followed by horses before 1000 BCE and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>, whose arrival reflects the growth of trans-Saharan trade.</p><p>European study of Algerian rock art began in 1863. Westerners were widely introduced to Tassili through sketches by French legionnaires, particularly Lieutenant Charles Brenans in the 1930s, who brought the archaeologist Henri Lhote. Lhote returned several times between 1956 and 1970. His expeditions have been heavily criticised, his team being accused of faking images and of damaging paintings by brightening them for tracing and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/photography">photography</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The rock art, first reported in 1910, includes about 15,000 engravings so far identified. The earliest pieces are presumed to be around 12,000 years old, although sources vary considerably, and most date to between 9,000 and 10,000 years ago or later. The subjects are large wild animals such as antelopes and crocodiles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> herds, and humans hunting and dancing. The art falls into five traditions: Archaic, Round Head, Bovidian or Pastoral, Horse and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">Camel</a>. The Round Head paintings include some of the oldest and largest exposed rock paintings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, with figures over five metres tall, and their concentration in particular places suggests ritual centres.</p><p>The Horse period shows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> and riders beside Libyan-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> inscriptions that remain undeciphered. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">Camel</a> period coincides with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> that carried salt, goods and enslaved people across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tassili n'Ajjer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Kel Ajjer emerged as a distinct confederation of the eastern Tuareg, occupying the <b>Tassili n'Ajjer</b> plateau and the oases of Ghat and Djanet in present-day south-eastern Algeria and south-western Libya.</blockquote><a href="/state/kel-ajjer">Kel Ajjer</a></li></ul></section>
